WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.393
Here is the older way of shipping software.

2
00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:06.959
One team writes the code.

3
00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:12.507
A separate operations team, called ops, installs it on servers and keeps it running.

4
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:16.261
When the app breaks at two in the morning, ops gets the call.

5
00:00:16.261 --> 00:00:18.805
But only the developers understand the code.

6
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:20.800
Each side blames the other.

7
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.465
So releases get rare and huge, and everyone dreads them.

8
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.279
DevOps removes that split.

9
00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.248
The people who build software also share the work of running it.

10
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:31.751
It has three parts.

11
00:00:31.751 --> 00:00:39.184
A shared culture, practices like small changes and automated tests, and tools that make those practices cheap.

12
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:47.151
A team that installs Kubernetes but keeps the wall between dev and ops ends up with the same wall, plus Kubernetes.

13
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:51.070
Every change takes the same road to your users.

14
00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.499
Engineers call that road the pipeline.

15
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.329
Take hello-devops, the tiny web app you create tomorrow.

16
00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:01.735
You change its default greeting from "Hello, DevOps!" to "Hi there".

17
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:03.151
Step one is code.

18
00:01:03.151 --> 00:01:06.844
You change code on your laptop, and commit it to git.

19
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.107
Step two, build.

20
00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:12.008
A machine turns the source code into something that can run.

21
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:13.354
Step three, test.

22
00:01:13.354 --> 00:01:17.836
Automated tests check that the change works, and broke nothing else.

23
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.343
And here, the test step fails at once, because one test still expects the old greeting.

24
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:29.120
You find out two minutes later, on your own branch, instead of from a user on Friday night.

25
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:35.548
Each step of the pipeline catches a different kind of mistake, as close as possible to where it was made.

26
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:39.805
Update that test, push again, and this time the change moves on.

27
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.703
Step four, package.

28
00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:48.788
The app and everything it needs go into one bundle, a container image, so it runs the same on any machine.

29
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.223
Step five, release.

30
00:01:50.223 --> 00:01:55.435
The package gets a version number, and goes to a shared store, called a registry.

31
00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:56.910
Step six, deploy.

32
00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.715
The new version replaces the old one on the servers.

33
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:01.991
Step seven, run.

34
00:02:01.991 --> 00:02:04.898
Software on the servers keeps the app alive.

35
00:02:04.898 --> 00:02:09.721
It restarts it if it crashes, and starts extra copies when traffic grows.

36
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:13.238
This is production, the real system your users use.

37
00:02:13.238 --> 00:02:16.155
Merging your code was only the halfway point.

38
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.797
Step eight, observe.

39
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:22.375
Graphs, logs and alerts show how the app behaves for real users.

40
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.818
Step nine, feedback.

41
00:02:23.818 --> 00:02:27.776
What you learn shapes the next change, and the loop starts again.

42
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.920
CI/CD covers steps two to six.

43
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:37.634
A machine builds and tests every small change, and keeps each one that passes ready to release.

44
00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:39.401
You build both in Part 6.

45
00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:41.805
Now, the course itself.

46
00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:45.153
Each of its 20 parts owns one piece of that path.

47
00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:48.083
Git, in Part 2, keeps every change.

48
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.858
GitHub Actions, in Part 6, tests and builds on every push.

49
00:02:52.858 --> 00:02:57.189
Gradle, in Part 7, builds, tests and packages a Kotlin app.

50
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:03.232
Docker, in Part 5, packages the app into an image that runs the same everywhere.

51
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:07.772
And GitHub Actions also publishes the image on every push.

52
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.853
Kubernetes, in Part 10, runs the containers across a cluster, and keeps them healthy.

53
00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:19.664
Argo CD, in Part 11, deploys by making the cluster match what git says.

54
00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:26.701
Prometheus, Grafana, and the logs and traces of Parts 12 to 14 tell you how it behaves.

55
00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:30.783
Terraform, in Part 8, creates the ground it runs on.

56
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:33.924
Ansible, in Part 9, sets up the servers on it.

57
00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:38.748
DevSecOps, in Part 17, adds security checks at every step.

58
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:42.994
Underneath everything sit Linux and networking, Parts 1 and 3.

59
00:03:42.994 --> 00:03:47.151
Every server and container runs Linux, and talks over a network.

60
00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:52.451
Python scripts and AI assistants, Parts 4 and 18, help at every layer.

61
00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.551
Do not try to memorize the list.

62
00:03:54.551 --> 00:04:00.147
When a new tool appears, in this course or at work, come back here, and ask which step it owns.

63
00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:04.388
One change flows from code to users, and comes back as feedback.

64
00:04:04.388 --> 00:04:07.655
Each part of this course owns one step of that loop.
